1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,720
At BITS Pilani Hyderabad Campus, over six thousand students, faculty, and administrative staff rely on complex digital infrastructure every single day for academic, research, and residential operations.

2
00:00:16,233 --> 00:00:30,353
When core fiber lines are damaged or central authentication clusters halt during midsem examinations, campuses experience catastrophic operational paralysis and cascading system downtime.

3
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:40,507
Traditional campus helpdesks fail under storm conditions, lacking deterministic state machines, automated correlation engines, and verifiable cryptographic audit trails.

4
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:53,073
Welcome to Incident-Zero: a military-grade, reactive IT service management platform engineered from the ground up for zero cascading downtime and absolute institutional resilience.

5
00:00:53,900 --> 00:01:05,060
Incident-Zero was conceived, designed, and implemented by Group 76 under the Object-Oriented Programming curriculum at BITS Pilani, Hyderabad Campus.

6
00:01:05,567 --> 00:01:15,207
Srijan Shukla architected the pure domain core, the eleven-state deterministic finite state machine, and the tamper-evident cryptographic audit ledger.

7
00:01:15,733 --> 00:01:27,453
Suhani Agarwal spearheaded the precision dual-clock SLA chronometer engine, business calendar arithmetic, and multi-tier escalation policies.

8
00:01:27,967 --> 00:01:36,807
Kartikey Negi engineered the campus service dependency directed acyclic graph, Tarjan cycle validation, and major incident storm correlation.

9
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:47,533
Hairsh Kumar crafted the full-stack responsive interface, interactive Three.js hardware telemetry, and enterprise REST controller adapters.

10
00:01:48,033 --> 00:02:01,833
Our platform adheres strictly to Ports and Adapters, or Hexagonal Architecture, isolating pure business entities completely from frameworks, persistence, and external drivers.

11
00:02:02,333 --> 00:02:12,453
Eleven discrete domain modules are governed by Spring Modulith, running atop high-throughput Java 22 virtual threads and cloud-native PostgreSQL.

12
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:26,127
The frontend combines modern React 19 and Tailwind CSS with a distinctive 16-bit pixel-arcade design language, making operational monitoring engaging and intuitive.

13
00:02:26,633 --> 00:02:39,073
Bi-directional Server-Sent Events stream live status updates across all connected stations, ensuring that student reports and technician actions remain synchronized with zero polling.

14
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:50,020
To guarantee long-term maintainability, the codebase enforces nine automated ArchUnit architectural rules that run on every compilation pass.

15
00:02:50,533 --> 00:03:01,133
Architecture Rule R1 establishes that domain classes contain zero imports of Spring, JPA, Hibernate, or Jackson, preserving pure Java semantics.

16
00:03:01,633 --> 00:03:13,273
Rule R5 strictly prohibits non-deterministic calls to Instant now and UUID randomUUID inside domain and application services, mandating mockable clock and identity ports.

17
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:25,600
Rule R3 dictates that transactional audit and SLA listeners must execute synchronously with mandatory propagation, guaranteeing zero-orphan ledger entries.

18
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:37,500
Every continuous integration run verifies all nine rules in under three seconds, catching architectural drift before any pull request can be merged.

19
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:49,160
A rigorous domain exception hierarchy maps fifteen strongly typed campus errors to standard RFC-7807 problem details with human-readable diagnostic remedies.

20
00:03:49,667 --> 00:04:04,267
Codes range from ITSM-4001 for illegal state machine transitions up to ITSM-5090 for optimistic concurrency locking conflicts.

21
00:04:04,767 --> 00:04:14,327
Global exception translation ensures that sensitive backend stack traces and credentials never leak to client browsers or terminal logs.

22
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:27,193
These strict invariants ensure that our domain model remains rock-solid, fully testable without database containers, and compiler-verified across half a million test vectors.

23
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:37,660
Spring Modulith continuously validates that package boundaries between incident, sla, audit, and assignment remain strictly unidirectional.

24
00:04:38,467 --> 00:04:47,067
We now demonstrate Core Workflow 1, following an incident through all eleven finite states from initial report to cryptographic sealing.

25
00:04:47,567 --> 00:04:55,687
Student Aarav Sharma logs into the self-service portal to report a critical failure on the SWD Mess and Fee Payment Gateway.

26
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:05,760
The student specifies high impact and high urgency. Our automated Priority Engine evaluates the campus calendar and notes an active fee payment window.

27
00:05:06,267 --> 00:05:14,627
Because the service carries the FEE_DEADLINE calendar tag, the priority calculation automatically bumps the ticket to P1 Critical.

28
00:05:15,133 --> 00:05:27,253
A full priority explanation JSON array is embedded into the ticket, explaining the exact formula, calendar modifiers, and institutional rules applied.

29
00:05:27,767 --> 00:05:37,567
The ticket is routed via the Least-Loaded Strategy to Senior Technician Vikram Rao, moving state to ASSIGNED and starting the response clock.

30
00:05:38,067 --> 00:05:50,667
Vikram clicks Acknowledge in the workbench. The initial response clock stops in two minutes, well under the five-minute P1 threshold.

31
00:05:51,167 --> 00:06:01,927
Remediating the payment gateway, Vikram applies a database pool restart and enters detailed resolution notes exceeding the mandatory twenty-character validation.

32
00:06:02,433 --> 00:06:13,033
The incident enters RESOLVED. Aarav receives a real-time notification, tests payment access, and confirms resolution with a five-star rating.

33
00:06:13,533 --> 00:06:23,173
Finally, the IT Administrator archives the verified ticket into CLOSED status, immutably sealing its resolution record in the audit ledger.

34
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:34,360
In enterprise operations, Service Level Agreements are legally binding commitments. Incident-Zero implements a dual-clock chronometer system.

35
00:06:34,867 --> 00:06:49,707
Across four service tiers and four priority levels, sixteen distinct SLA policies govern response targets from five minutes to resolution targets of three days.

36
00:06:50,233 --> 00:06:59,953
Tier zero and tier one services run on continuous twenty-four-seven clocks, while peripheral services operate under standard business hours.

37
00:07:00,467 --> 00:07:11,627
Our dynamic chronometer evaluates time consumption, firing visual warnings at fifty percent, seventy-five percent, and ninety percent intervals.

38
00:07:12,133 --> 00:07:23,213
If a resolution target lapses, the ticket marks a sticky breach. Once breached, the status remains permanently attached to the ticket history.

39
00:07:23,733 --> 00:07:33,533
Breaching ninety percent automatically triggers the multi-tier Escalation Ladder, notifying team leads, IT managers, and system administrators.

40
00:07:34,033 --> 00:07:43,353
When a ticket transitions to PENDING_USER for student feedback, the resolution clock cleanly suspends and resumes upon user reply.

41
00:07:43,867 --> 00:07:54,787
A monotonic clock generation counter ensures that whenever an SLA timer pauses or updates, legacy in-flight async timer events are safely invalidated.

42
00:07:55,300 --> 00:08:03,900
Business hours arithmetic automatically excludes official BITS holidays like Gandhi Jayanti, Dussehra, and Diwali from timer countdowns.

43
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:13,000
Comprehensive unit tests prove that clock pauses, resumptions, and holiday skips maintain microsecond accuracy across month-long windows.

44
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:23,120
Campus IT services do not operate in a vacuum. Core infrastructure failures produce massive, compounding blast radiuses.

45
00:08:23,633 --> 00:08:32,153
Incident-Zero models all nineteen campus services across twenty-six hard and soft dependency edges as a directed acyclic graph.

46
00:08:32,667 --> 00:08:43,347
Tarjan's strongly connected components algorithm executes during graph updates, rejecting any accidental circular dependencies at runtime.

47
00:08:43,867 --> 00:08:53,347
When Data Center cooling fails, core switches overheat, and Central LDAP goes down, hundreds of student login tickets flood the portal.

48
00:08:53,867 --> 00:09:02,147
Our Storm Detection Engine activates, tracking ticket velocity over five-minute sliding windows against the dependency topology.

49
00:09:02,667 --> 00:09:14,067
The system automatically pinpoints Central LDAP as the root cause, declaring a Major Incident and linking forty-five tickets as child duplicates.

50
00:09:14,567 --> 00:09:21,887
Chief Administrator Suresh Deshmukh is appointed Incident Commander, locking triage roles and broadcasting campus-wide alerts.

51
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:33,720
The interactive 3D blast radius visualizer highlights downstream services at risk, including Moodle LMS, AUGSD ERP, and hostel Wi-Fi.

52
00:09:34,233 --> 00:09:51,073
Transactional cascade listeners propagate the root resolution notes down to all child incidents, preserving audit integrity for each student.

53
00:09:51,600 --> 00:10:00,840
When the Commander resolves the root LDAP ticket, Incident-Zero automatically resolves all linked duplicate tickets and notifies affected students.

54
00:10:01,667 --> 00:10:10,827
To reduce technician load, Incident-Zero integrates fully offline, local intelligence for automated ticket deflection and dispatch.

55
00:10:11,333 --> 00:10:20,973
Incident titles and knowledge base articles are transformed into 384-dimensional dense vectors using an embedded ONNX runtime.

56
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:33,140
PostgreSQL pgvector with Hierarchical Navigable Small World indexes enables sub-millisecond approximate nearest neighbor searches.

57
00:10:33,667 --> 00:10:43,147
When a student begins typing an issue with campus Wi-Fi, verified articles on CAS cookie resets appear instantly, deflecting redundant tickets.

58
00:10:43,667 --> 00:10:55,307
For unresolved tickets, seven pluggable Routing Strategies evaluate technician shifts, verified skill badges, and active point loads.

59
00:10:55,833 --> 00:11:04,113
Skill-based routing matches Aruba Wi-Fi and BIND DNS certifications, ensuring specialized problems land with certified engineers.

60
00:11:04,633 --> 00:11:13,473
Load tracking enforces maximum point capacities per technician, guaranteeing balanced distribution across morning and evening shifts.

61
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:22,680
Atomic load tracking prevents race conditions under high concurrency, using database row versions to guarantee fairness.

62
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:31,800
Automated test suites confirm zero routing race conditions even under concurrent ticket bursts from hundreds of client threads.

63
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:44,000
Accountability requires unalterable proof. Incident-Zero guarantees non-repudiation through a cryptographically linked audit ledger.

64
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:55,740
Every ticket change computes a SHA-256 digest over the sequence index, timestamp, actor ID, action name, payload, and the prior hash.

65
00:11:56,267 --> 00:12:04,707
The admin console displays the unbroken cryptographic hash chain for every ticket, allowing one-click verification of history.

66
00:12:05,233 --> 00:12:13,193
At the database level, an append-only trigger immediately rejects any UPDATE, DELETE, or TRUNCATE statement on audit records.

67
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:21,500
If a malicious actor alters a single byte directly in the database, the cryptographic verification algorithm flags the chain as tampered.

68
00:12:22,033 --> 00:12:32,313
Authentication is powered by RFC-9106 Argon2id password hashing and cryptographically signed stateless JWT access tokens.

69
00:12:32,833 --> 00:12:40,473
Strict Role-Based Access Control isolates student self-service from technician queues and executive administrative governance.

70
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:49,440
Tokens carry cryptographically verified claims, preventing privilege escalation between Employee, Technician, and Administrator roles.

71
00:12:49,967 --> 00:12:56,887
Real-time updates stream securely over Server-Sent Events using short-lived, single-use stream tickets to prevent ticket hijacking.

72
00:12:57,700 --> 00:13:08,780
A premier objective of this course project was translating classical object-oriented design principles into enterprise reality.

73
00:13:09,300 --> 00:13:18,620
The Strategy Pattern abstracts incident assignment algorithms, allowing runtime polymorphism between Round Robin and Least Loaded routing.

74
00:13:19,133 --> 00:13:28,373
The State Pattern encapsulates all eleven lifecycle states, isolating valid transitions and eliminating fragile conditional branching.

75
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:38,700
The Observer Pattern, powered by Spring Modulith event publication, decouples SLA timers, audit logging, and email notifications.

76
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:49,400
The Builder Pattern enforces strict entity construction invariants, calculating initial SLA deadlines and priority ratings atomically.

77
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:59,460
The Decorator Pattern layers multi-channel notifications, wrapping base email alerts with in-app notifications and SMS broadcasts.

78
00:13:59,967 --> 00:14:09,687
The Adapter Pattern bridges domain repositories with Spring Data JPA entities, protecting domain logic from database schema changes.

79
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:18,480
The Facade Pattern provides clean, high-level interfaces for controllers, coordinating complex multi-module transactions seamlessly.

80
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:27,360
The Template Method Pattern governs calendar calculations, providing a rigid skeleton for holiday checks while allowing variable shift windows.

81
00:14:27,867 --> 00:14:38,547
Together, these eleven patterns exemplify SOLID principles, yielding a codebase that is deeply modular, extensible, and easy to maintain.

82
00:14:39,367 --> 00:14:48,287
Engineering excellence requires exhaustive verification. Our test harness spans unit tests, integration slices, and state machine fuzzing.

83
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:58,440
Over five hundred and twenty thousand valid and invalid transition permutations are checked to prove zero deadlocks exist in our state model.

84
00:14:58,967 --> 00:15:09,887
Deterministic testing is powered by our automated reset script, which recreates the full campus schema and seeds forty-five tickets in 2.14 seconds.

85
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:20,440
End-to-end integration tests execute complete multi-user workflows headlessly, verifying token passing, clock ticks, and audit hashing.

86
00:15:20,967 --> 00:15:29,087
Every custom exception, from validation constraints to database deadlock retries, is verified under heavy concurrent load.

87
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:37,640
On the frontend, Playwright end-to-end specs ensure responsive layout rendering across 1080p desktop, tablet, and mobile viewports.

88
00:15:38,167 --> 00:15:46,527
Global exception translation ensures that sensitive backend stack traces never leak to client browsers or terminal logs.

89
00:15:47,033 --> 00:15:54,273
Our test suite delivers ninety-four percent statement coverage across all domain aggregates and critical business pathways.

90
00:15:54,800 --> 00:16:05,240
The entire verification pipeline runs completely locally, offline, and deterministically without external network dependencies.

91
00:16:06,067 --> 00:16:14,747
In summary, Incident-Zero fully achieves every technical objective and specification articulated in the BITS Pilani course brief.

92
00:16:15,267 --> 00:16:25,867
All nine technology report questions on concurrency, database integrity, architectural tradeoffs, and performance have been empirically answered.

93
00:16:26,367 --> 00:16:35,847
Our concurrency report demonstrates how optimistic database locks combined with virtual threads prevent phantom updates under peak student load.

94
00:16:36,367 --> 00:16:52,247
By uniting rigorous object-oriented design with modern reactive architecture, we transformed campus IT from a reactive bottleneck into a resilient backbone.

95
00:16:52,767 --> 00:17:04,247
On behalf of Group 76 — Srijan Shukla, Suhani Agarwal, Kartikey Negi, and Hairsh Kumar — thank you for your time and evaluation.

96
00:17:04,767 --> 00:17:13,047
The complete source code, architectural decision records, and automated test suites are publicly accessible on GitHub.
